WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
Here is a stretch of DNA, 18 base pairs long.

2
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.980
A cell copies part of it into RNA.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.360
Which strand does RNA polymerase read, and which way does it go?

4
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.880
Look at the bare duplex.

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
Nothing in it says.

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.000
You need the RNA that was made, or you need a promoter.

7
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:28.393
First, the rules of the reaction.

8
00:00:29.473 --> 00:00:32.933
RNA polymerase opens a short stretch of the helix.

9
00:00:33.453 --> 00:00:35.493
It uses one strand as a guide.

10
00:00:36.193 --> 00:00:38.153
That strand is the template strand.

11
00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:46.633
The enzyme lines up RNA letters by base pairing, with U opposite A. It joins each

12
00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:49.713
new letter to the 3′ hydroxyl of the chain.

13
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:53.473
So the RNA grows 5′ to 3′.

14
00:00:53.473 --> 00:00:56.993
The RNA is antiparallel to its template.

15
00:00:57.413 --> 00:01:01.073
So the polymerase reads the template 3′ to 5′.

16
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.787
Now use the real RNA.

17
00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:18.147
It reads A, C, G, U, U, U, C, A, U, G, G. The RNA has the same sequence as the

18
00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:25.407
coding strand, with U in place of T. So look for that sequence, reading 5′ to 3′.

19
00:01:25.407 --> 00:01:28.187
Read the bottom strand from right to left.

20
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:40.227
It spells A, C, G, T, T, T, C, A, T, G, G. That matches.

21
00:01:40.887 --> 00:01:42.907
The top strand does not contain it.

22
00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:45.387
So the bottom strand is the coding strand.

23
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.760
The template is the other strand, the top one.

24
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.720
Check it by pairing.

25
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.200
The RNA lies antiparallel to the top strand.

26
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.580
The first RNA letter, A, pairs with the top strand's T, at the right end.

27
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.660
C pairs with G, G with C, and so on leftward.

28
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:09.400
Every letter pairs.

29
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.060
The top strand is the template.

30
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:15.153
Now watch the enzyme work.

31
00:02:15.953 --> 00:02:19.793
The polymerase reads the template 3′ to 5′.

32
00:02:20.213 --> 00:02:23.073
On the top strand, 3′ is at the right.

33
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:26.713
So the polymerase moves right to left along the page.

34
00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:32.273
The RNA grows 5′ to 3′ in that same leftward direction.

35
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:36.033
Its 5′ end lies toward the right of the duplex.

36
00:02:36.866 --> 00:02:40.066
Two checks catch most mistakes.

37
00:02:40.946 --> 00:02:44.346
The RNA must be antiparallel to its template.

38
00:02:45.106 --> 00:02:51.326
And a bare duplex, with no RNA and no promoter, does not tell you which strand is

39
00:02:51.326 --> 00:02:53.886
the template. So find the RNA.

40
00:02:54.786 --> 00:03:00.566
Keep this rule: the RNA matches the coding strand and pairs with the template.
